Netlify is a platform for building, deploying, and hosting web projects, and its [API](https://docs.netlify.com/api-and-cli-guides/api-guides/get-started-with-api/) lets other apps manage a user's sites, deploys, and forms. If you're building for Netlify users, such as a deploy dashboard, a CMS, or a developer tool, Sign in with Netlify gives your app the user and a Netlify API token in one step.

# How does the integration work?

Appwrite Auth has a Netlify OAuth2 provider built on Netlify's authorization code flow. The session stores Netlify's access token as `providerAccessToken`, so your server can call the Netlify API on the user's behalf.

Appwrite doesn't request any scopes from Netlify. It reads the user's profile from the Netlify API and stores their full name and email address, and their Netlify user ID becomes the provider user ID on the user's [identity](/docs/products/auth/identities). The avatar is stored on the identity too, so it also powers [user photos](/docs/products/avatars/user-photos) in Appwrite Avatars.

Netlify doesn't report whether the email address is verified, so Appwrite stores it as unverified. If another user in your project already has the same address, Appwrite redirects to your failure URL instead of signing in to that account. To connect Netlify to an existing account, have the user sign in first and then start `createOAuth2Session` from the browser, which links Netlify as a new [identity](/docs/products/auth/identities).

{% info title="Self-hosted availability" %}
Sign in with Netlify is live on Appwrite Cloud and ships in the next self-hosted Appwrite release.
{% /info %}

# How to implement

To add Netlify sign-in to your app, complete these steps.

## Step 1: Copy the redirect URI from Appwrite

For this step, you must [create an account on Appwrite Cloud](/sign-up) or [self-host Appwrite](/docs/advanced/self-hosting) if you haven't already.

In the Appwrite Console, open your project, go to **Auth** > **Social providers**, and open **Netlify**. Copy the **Redirect URI** shown in the dialog. It includes the provider ID and your project ID:

```text
https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/account/sessions/oauth2/callback/netlify/<PROJECT_ID>
```

The URI starts with the API endpoint your app uses, so on self-hosted Appwrite it starts with your own domain. Always copy the exact value from the Console.

## Step 2: Register a Netlify OAuth application

Open your Netlify [user settings for applications](https://app.netlify.com/user/applications) and, under **OAuth applications**, select **New OAuth app**. Give the application a name, paste the redirect URI from step 1 as the redirect URI, and save it. Netlify then shows the application's **Client ID** and **Secret**.

Treat the secret like a password. If it's ever exposed, replace the OAuth application and update the credentials in Appwrite.

## Step 3: Enable the provider in Appwrite

Back in **Auth** > **Social providers**, open **Netlify**. Paste the Client ID into **Client ID** and the secret into **Client Secret**, turn the provider on, and select **Update**.

## Step 4: Add Netlify sign-in to your app

Pass `OAuthProvider.Netlify` as the provider when you start the OAuth2 flow. The value is in the Node.js server SDK from `node-appwrite` 32.0.0. Until your client SDK includes it, start the flow from your server with `createOAuth2Token` and exchange the returned `userId` and `secret` for a session, as described in [server-side OAuth2 login](/docs/products/auth/server-side-rendering#oauth2). Add your app's domain as a platform in your Appwrite project so Appwrite accepts your `success` and `failure` URLs.

# Things to know about Netlify sign-in

- **The token acts as the user.** Netlify's OAuth flow lets users authorize your app to use Netlify on their behalf, and Appwrite doesn't request any narrower scopes. Keep `providerAccessToken` on your server and never send it to the browser.
- **A password reset revokes tokens.** When a user resets their Netlify password, Netlify invalidates every OAuth token issued before the reset. Send the user through sign-in again if Netlify API calls start returning authorization errors.
- **The `scopes` parameter has no effect.** Appwrite doesn't send scopes to Netlify, so values passed in `scopes` are ignored.

